---
layout: base.njk
---
{#- The h1 carries a visually-hidden "The claim: " prefix, on the same idiom lib/citation.mjs and
    lib/charts.mjs use for their summaries. The visible label sits above the heading and a sighted
    reader meets the two together, but a screen-reader user navigating BY HEADING meets the h1
    alone, and "Most immigration is asylum seekers." on its own reads as this site asserting it.
    That is the same defect as an unqualified share card, arriving by a route a screenshot cannot.
    The h1 is the claim itself, so every claim page has a distinctive heading for search,
    browser tabs and heading-by-heading navigation. It sits INSIDE the card and under its
    "The claim" label, so the framing travels with it and a bare screenshot of the heading
    can never read as this site asserting the claim. See foundation section 8.5.4. -#}
{#- The paused state, backlog call 28. `/sources-and-method/` promises that a claim unreviewed for
    twelve months is taken down until it has been, and there was no state for that: the options in
    the check's own error message were re-review or unpublish, and on a static site unpublishing
    means deleting a file, which turns a URL built to be screenshotted into a generic 404. A 404
    reads as scrubbing, which is the accusation this site is least able to afford.

    So a paused claim keeps its address and says what it said and why it is paused. What it does
    NOT keep is the answer, the figures and the citation: those are the thing the promise takes
    down, and leaving them up behind an explanation would keep the promise in words only.

    The claim text stays inside the card under its "The claim" label, for the same reason the live
    version does, foundation 8.5.4: a screenshot of the heading can never read as this site
    asserting the sentence.

    Pausing is a deliberate act and not a timeout. The twelve-month check still fails the build,
    which is what makes someone look; this is the third answer to it, beside re-reviewing and
    deleting. Making it automatic would trade a check that stops a deploy for a page that quietly
    empties itself, and nothing would say it had happened. -#}
{% if paused %}
<article class="claim claim-paused">
  <div class="claim-card">
    {#- aria-hidden because the h1 below now carries "The claim: " in its own accessible name.
        Without this a screen reader reading linearly announces the label twice, once here and
        once in the heading. The visible label stays exactly where it is; only the duplicate
        announcement goes. -#}
    <p class="claim-label" aria-hidden="true">The claim</p>
    <h1 class="claim-text"><span class="visually-hidden">The claim: </span>{{ claim }}</h1>
    {#- The reason is stated only where the page states one. An earlier version of this sentence
        said the twelve-month promise was why THIS page came down, and nothing made that true:
        `paused` is a date and a claim can be taken down at any age, a retracted source being the
        obvious case. That would have put a confident false cause on a site whose subject is
        confident false causes. Where no reason is given the page says what it knows, which is
        that the answer is down pending re-review. -#}
    <dl class="claim-answer-list">
      <dt class="claim-label">This check is paused</dt>
      <dd class="claim-answer">The answer and the figures that were on this page came down on
      {{ paused | timeTag | safe }}, pending re-review. It was last reviewed on
      {{ last_reviewed | timeTag | safe }}.{% if paused_reason %} {{ paused_reason }}{% endif %}</dd>
    </dl>
    <dl class="claim-meta">
      <div><dt>Reviewed</dt> <dd>{{ last_reviewed | timeTag | safe }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Paused</dt> <dd>{{ paused | timeTag | safe }}</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <p>The page stays at this address rather than disappearing, so a link taken when the check was
  live still leads somewhere that says what happened to it. This site publishes no claim check it
  has not reviewed within twelve months, which is the standing promise a page can come down under;
  the <a href="/sources-and-method/#corrections">corrections policy</a> states it, and it is not
  necessarily why this page came down.</p>

  <p class="back"><a href="/common-claims/">All common claims</a></p>
</article>
{% else %}
<article class="claim">
  <div class="claim-card">
    {#- aria-hidden because the h1 below now carries "The claim: " in its own accessible name.
        Without this a screen reader reading linearly announces the label twice, once here and
        once in the heading. The visible label stays exactly where it is; only the duplicate
        announcement goes. "The short answer" below is NOT hidden: nothing else carries it. -#}
    <p class="claim-label" aria-hidden="true">The claim</p>
    <h1 class="claim-text"><span class="visually-hidden">The claim: </span>{{ claim }}</h1>
    {#- A label with one value: a description list, the label keeping its class so it looks as
        it did. -#}
    <dl class="claim-answer-list">
      <dt class="claim-label">The short answer</dt>
      <dd class="claim-answer">{{ short_answer }}</dd>
    </dl>
    {#- The site name was here as a byline. The header, the footer and the browser tab all
        carry it already, and inside the card it read as one more undifferentiated item in
        a row that needed fewer, not more. -#}
    {#- Three labelled values. The labels replaced the aria-hidden dot separators on 22 September
        2026: a period and a publisher set side by side with no label read as one phrase, and a
        description list names each. -#}
    <dl class="claim-meta">
      <div><dt>Reviewed</dt> <dd>{{ last_reviewed | timeTag | safe }}</dd></div>
      {% if period %}<div><dt>Period</dt> <dd>{{ period }}</dd></div>{% endif %}
      {% if source %}<div><dt>Source</dt> <dd>{{ source }}</dd></div>{% endif %}
    </dl>
    {#- Inside the card, so that a screenshot of the card carries the citation with it. The
        publications a reader should cite are the Home Office's and the ONS's, not this page. -#}
    {% citation figures, claim %}
  </div>

  {#- Two labels with one value each: a description list, styled as pills. -#}
  <dl class="claim-tags">
    <div class="tag"><dt>Misuse</dt> <dd>{{ error_type }}</dd></div>
    <div class="tag"><dt>Direction</dt> <dd>{{ direction }}</dd></div>
  </dl>

  {#- A substantive revision is disclosed on the claim it changed, not only in the
      changelog, because a reader of this page will not go looking for the changelog. See
      the corrections policy on the sources page. -#}
  {#- A div with role="note" and a real heading, not an aside and not a bold run-in: the
      correction is part of the page's content and belongs in its outline. -#}
  {% if correction %}
  <div class="correction" role="note">
    <h2>Correction, {{ corrected_on | timeTag | safe }}</h2>
    <p>{{ correction }}</p>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {{ content | safe }}

  <p class="back"><a href="/common-claims/">All common claims</a></p>
</article>
{% endif %}
